# The Required and Recommended rules of the same name hold every other
# fancy word. Each word here also appears in plain human-written docs,
# so a finding suggests a change rather than asking for one.
#
# `enhance` hit 40 lines of the measure.py corpus, and the opener
# `Additionally,` hit 63. `enhanced` stays out, because it opens
# feature names (`Enhanced Networking`, `Security Enhanced Linux`).
# `additionally` counts only with a glued comma, because mid-sentence
# it modifies a verb (`the flag additionally checks the signature`).
#
# `Moreover` and `furthermore` join `Additionally,` here, because the
# two hit 24 lines of the same corpus. Neither modifies a verb, so both
# count without the comma. `facilitate` sits here too, because it hit 20
# lines (`to facilitate testing`).
#
# `as per` and `per` before `the`, `its`, `your`, or `their` stand in
# for `according to` (`per the setting`), and hit 19 lines. `per` alone
# stays out, because it also names a rate (`requests per user`). `As
# such,` stands in for `so` and hit 25 lines. It counts only with its
# comma, because without one it names a role (`the value is treated as
# such`).
#
# It hit 191 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 20 of 20 sampled hits
# stood in for a plainer word.
expr main [enhance, enhances, enhancing, (additionally,), moreover, furthermore, facilitate, facilitates, facilitated, facilitating, (as per), (per [the, its, your, their]), (as such,)]
let message "Fancy word. Write `improve` or `extend` for `enhance`, `according to` for `as per` or `per the`, and `so` for `as such`. State what the subject does instead of `facilitate`. Delete `additionally`, `moreover`, or `furthermore`, or write `also`."
let description "Flags `enhance`, `facilitate`, `as per`, `per the`, and the openers `additionally`, `moreover`, `furthermore`, and `as such`, which stand in for plainer words."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "The enhancement label marks feature requests."
allows "The flag additionally checks the signature."
allows "The quota counts requests per user."
